    "q-97969": {
      "dom_id": "step-q97969",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "If $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(A-3I)=4,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\text{nullity}(A-3I)=0$\n\nII. $A-3I$ is invertible\n\nIII. $\\lambda =3$ is an eigenvalue of $A$",
      "worked_solution": "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $4$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(A-3I) & =4-\\text{rank}(A-3I) \\\\  & =4-4 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A-3I)=0,$ we obtain that $A-3I$ must be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that if $M=A-3I$ is invertible, then $(A-3I)x=0$ has only zero solution. As a result, $\\lambda =3$ can not be an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
