    "q-140838": {
      "dom_id": "step-q140838",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\lambda =-4$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $(A+4I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution\n\nII. $\\text{Col}(A+4I)={R}^{2}$\n\nIII. $\\text{nullity}(A+4I)=0$",
      "worked_solution": "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=A+4I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =4$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A+4I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, the matrix $M=A+4I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are false. Since $M=A+4I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(A+4I)\\ne {R}^{2},\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A+4I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
